---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Legal from '../components/Legal.astro';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../consts';
---

<Base lang="en" title="Privacy Policy — P-Pass" mirror="/zh/privacy/">
  <Legal title="Privacy Policy" updated="2026-10-26" updatedLabel="Last updated: ">
    <p>
      This policy covers the P-Pass website (p-pass.hawkeye-xb.com), the P-Pass desktop app, and the P-Pass apps for Android and iPhone (iOS). It explains what data we can see and what we can't.
    </p>

    <h2>Your photos and videos</h2>
    <p>
      Your photos and videos are sent from your phone to your own computer and stored on the computer where you installed P-Pass. We don't run servers that store user photos, and we have no way to view your photos.
    </p>

    <h2>Transfers and the relay</h2>
    <p>
      Connections between your devices are encrypted end to end. When your phone and computer can't reach each other directly, the encrypted data is forwarded through a relay server. The relay and device-discovery services currently in use are operated by number 0, the maintainers of the open-source networking library iroh. These services can see your devices' IP addresses and device identifiers (public keys). They can't see the content of your photos, and they don't store the data they forward.
    </p>
    <p>
      To let your devices find each other, each device's public key and its current network addresses are published to that public device-discovery service, so anyone who knows a device's public key can look up where it is reachable. The public key is not your name or account, and it says nothing about your photos.
    </p>

    <h2>Pairing and device names</h2>
    <p>
      When you pair a phone with your computer, the phone sends the name you gave it (for example "Mom's phone") to your computer. The name is shown on your computer so you can approve the pairing, and it's kept in your computer's device list and activity log. It is sent only to your own computer, never to us.
    </p>

    <h2>Data collected by the apps</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The desktop, Android and iOS apps currently send no usage data or analytics, and there is no automatic crash reporting.</li>
      <li>The code contains no telemetry or usage-statistics reporting of any kind. Diagnostic information is written only to logs on your own device, and you decide whether to export it and send it to us.</li>
      <li>If we ever need a crash log from you, we'll ask first.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Update checks</h2>
    <p>
      The Android app checks for new versions by downloading a small, signed manifest file (a static file hosted on GitHub Releases and on our Cloudflare download mirror). It's an ordinary HTTPS request: the host sees your IP address and the app's fixed name, "P-Pass-UpdateChecker", and the app sends no account, device identifier, or usage data with it.
    </p>

    <h2>Diagnostic bundles</h2>
    <p>
      A diagnostic bundle is created only when you click "Export diagnostic bundle", and it's saved on your computer. Exported bundles are scrubbed automatically: home-folder paths are replaced, and device identifiers and pairing tokens are cut to their first 8 characters. We only see a bundle if you send it to us yourself.
    </p>
    <p>
      The iOS app keeps its logs on your phone. Its diagnostics screen only lets you copy the app version and connection status, for example to paste into a bug report.
    </p>

    <h2>Android permissions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Photos and videos: to back up the albums you choose.</li>
      <li>Photo location metadata (Android's "access media location"): to read the shooting location stored inside your photos, so the backed-up original is byte-for-byte the same file. The app doesn't read your device's location.</li>
      <li>Camera: to scan the pairing QR code on your computer.</li>
      <li>Notifications: to show backup progress and tell you when a backup fails.</li>
      <li>Network, background activity, start on boot, and battery-optimization exemption: to finish backups automatically in the background.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>iOS permissions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Photos (full or limited access): to back up the photos and videos you allow.</li>
      <li>Add to Photos: to save a photo from your computer back to your iPhone when you ask for it.</li>
      <li>Camera: to scan the pairing QR code on your computer.</li>
      <li>Local network: to find your computer on the same Wi-Fi and transfer photos to it directly.</li>
      <li>Notifications: to tell you when a backup fails.</li>
      <li>Background tasks: to continue backups in the background when iOS allows it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The iOS app's pairing key is stored in the iPhone keychain and is not synced to iCloud. Pairing and backup records are excluded from iCloud backups.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      This website uses no analytics scripts, cookies, or third-party trackers. It is hosted on GitHub Pages, and installers are distributed through GitHub Releases, so GitHub processes access logs as the host under its
      <a href="https://github.com/site/privacy" rel="external">privacy statement</a>.
      The Chinese version of this site links to a download mirror on Cloudflare (p-pass-dl.hawkeye-xb.com); downloads from the mirror are logged by Cloudflare under its privacy policy. The download numbers we see are only the totals GitHub Releases and Cloudflare provide.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>When this policy changes, we update the "Last updated" date on this page. Any change to how data is collected will also be called out in the release notes.</p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions about this policy? Email <a href="mailto:ppass@hawkeye-xb.com">ppass@hawkeye-xb.com</a> or open an issue on <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/issues`} rel="external">GitHub Issues</a>.</p>
  </Legal>
</Base>
